          "path": "_m/s/w/e/r/temple-of-artemis-wp/remains-of-the-temples-of-arte.jpg",
          "filename": "Remains of the Temples of Artemes (16313271901).jpg",
          "caption": "Selcuk had one of the Seven Wonders of the World in the olden days, and this is what unfortunately remains of it now. The city was famed for the Temple of Artemis (completed around 550 BC), one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. In 268 AD, the Temple was destroyed or damaged in a raid by the Goths. It may have been rebuilt or repaired but this is uncertain, as its later history is not clear. Emperor Constantine I rebuilt much of the city and erected new public baths. Following the Edict of Thessalonica from emperor Theodosius I, what remained of the temple was destroyed in 401 AD by a mob led by St. John Chrysostom. The town was partially destroyed by an earthquake in 614 AD. The city's importance as a commercial cenrte declined as the harbour was slowly silted up by the Cayster River  (Ephesus (Efes)/ Selcuk, Turkey, Nov. 2014)",
